ouldn't be directly addressed  in the second fantastic beasts movie  the crimes of grindelwald despite the  fact that the antagonist of the film  was famously known as dumbledore's lover  on the movie's blu-ray release  rowling followed up the controversy by  announcing that the two  had indeed gauged in an extremely  intense physical relationship  but this reveal didn't come without its  controversy with rowling's comments  raising some important questions about  representation  after all what does it say to lgbtq fans  that dumbledore fell in love with a  murderous villain  and then possibly lived in celibacy for  the remainder of his life  and why is that representation found  only on a blu-ray special feature  rather than within the story itself all  in all this particular reveal sent a  more muddled and confusing message than  rowling likely intended  one of the most fascinating acts of  magic in the entire wizarding world  is the patronus charm a difficult and  advanced spell which summons a magical  protector that seems to come from  inside a wizard's own spirit harry first  discovers his patronus in prisoner of  azkaban  where it takes the form of a stag in  honor of harry's late father james who  could transform into that same animal  after the story officially ended fans  wanted to know about the patronuses of  their favorite characters  from hermione's otter to luna lovegood's  rabbit  but one revelation about a truly beloved  character was particularly tragic  when asked what patronus might belong to  hogwarts groundskeeper  rubius hagrid rowling responded that  hagrid wasn't able to produce the spell  at all  likely thanks to his wrongful expulsion  from hogwarts as a teenager  this wasn't rowling's only patronus  revelation either  the author also noted that severus snape  was the only death eater who could  summon a patronus  since he was the only member of the evil  group with his soul still intact  after all this time  always harry potter fans still have  plenty of lingering questions about the  wizarding world  and to her credit rolling has answered  many of them but she has also  answered a few questions nobody ever  asked despite bathrooms  playing a weirdly significant role in  the series fans weren't exactly  clamoring to find out how  wizards got along before the days of  indoor plumbing even so  rowling was on hand to clear that one up  so to speak in early 2019  a tweet from harry potter website  pottermore revealed a pretty unexpected  fact about the wizarding world  apparently before wizards began using  muggle style plumbing  it basically just went right on the  floor and then got rid of it  using a vanishing spell weirdly enough  this wasn't  actually new information either  pottermore had previously revealed this  in a 2015 essay on the website but the  2019 tweet  caused far more of a stir with many fans  wondering why it even needed to be said  every long-running series needs an  iconic villain and voldemort  clearly fit the bill for harry potter  after trying to kill harry as an infant  and nearly perishing himself voldemort  spends the rest of his life on a mission  to defeat harry once and for all when  harry hears a prophecy about the two in  order of the phoenix  he realizes that he can't avoid his fate  for neither  can live while the other survives  after destroying voldemort's horcruxes  and sacrificing himself to defeat the  evil wizard  harry is finally able to best voldemort  once and for all  taking down one of the most nefarious  and murderous wizards the world has ever  seen  in fact voldemort was so nefarious and  murderous that most wizards refused to  even speak his name  instead many of them simply referred to  him as he who must not be named  or you know who voldemort voldemort  as it turns out however even though it's  bold enough to say  voldemort have been getting it all wrong  in 2015  rowling revealed she always meant  voldemort to be pronounced in the french  fashion wit